UNZIP(1)                       P32W Unix                        UNZIP(1)

NAME
       unzip - list, test or extract a zip archive

SYNOPSIS
       unzip [-lqtoan] [-d dir] archive[.zip] [name ...]

DESCRIPTION
       unzip extracts the entries of a zip (PKZIP) archive: one made
       by zip(1), by the Mac's zip, or by Finder's Compress. If
       archive cannot be opened, or is a directory, archive.zip is
       tried.

       With names, only the entries with those names, and everything
       below them (name/...), are taken; otherwise all entries are.
       The __MACOSX/ entries that Finder adds (resource forks) are
       left out unless -a is given. A name that matches no entry is
       reported ("not found in the archive") at the end.

       Stored and deflated entries are understood; the CRC and size
       of each are checked. Directories are made as needed. A file
       gets the permission bits recorded in the archive if it was
       made on Unix, else mode 0644 (directories 0755). Its
       modification time comes from the "UT" or "UX" extra field if
       there is one, else from the DOS date and time, taken as local
       time by TZ. When unzip runs as root, files and directories
       also get the owner and group recorded in the archive (the
       "ux" extra field that zip(1) and Info-ZIP write, else the
       16-bit ones of "UX"), if it records them; others become the
       owner of what they extract. Directories get their modes,
       owners and times after everything else is extracted.

       A file that fails its CRC or size check, or whose data is
       damaged, is reported and removed. A symbolic link entry is
       reported and skipped, since P32W Unix has none.

       Before replacing an existing file unzip asks

              replace name? [y]es, [n]o, [A]ll, [N]one:

       when the standard input is a terminal; A answers yes and N no
       for all the files after it. When it is not a terminal the
       file is left alone, with a message, unless -o is given, and
       the exit status is 1.

       unzip prints "Archive:" and the archive's name, then a line
       per entry: "creating:" for a directory, "inflating:" or
       "extracting:" for a deflated or stored file.

OPTIONS
       -l     List the entries instead: size, date and time (local)
              and name, then the total size and the number of
              entries. Nothing is extracted.

       -t     Test: expand every entry and check its CRC, without
              writing anything; say "OK" for each and "No errors
              detected" at the end. Names select entries as for
              extraction.

       -q     Quiet: none of the progress lines.

       -o     Replace existing files without asking.

       -n     Never replace existing files; skip them silently (the
              exit status stays 0).

       -a     Also extract the __MACOSX/ entries.

       -d dir
              Extract into dir instead of the current directory. dir
              is made if it does not exist (one level only).

       Options may be combined (-qo) and must come before the
       archive name.

EXAMPLES
       unzip -l photos               list photos.zip
       unzip -t photos.zip           check it
       unzip -d /tmp/x src.zip       extract into /tmp/x
       unzip -o src.zip src/cmd      extract one subtree, replacing
       unzip -n /mnt/ken.zip         only the files not already there

EXIT STATUS
       0 if all went well; 1 if an entry could not be extracted (an
       encrypted, Zip64 or unknown-method entry, a symbolic link, an
       absolute name or one with .., a bad CRC or size, damaged
       data, a file that could not be created, an existing file not
       replaced without a terminal to ask at), or a name was not
       found in the archive; 2 for a usage error, an archive that
       cannot be opened, is not a zip archive or has a damaged
       central directory, or a -d dir that cannot be made.

SEE ALSO
       zip(1), tar(1), gzip(1), kermit(1), deflate(3), crc32(3)

BUGS
       There is no -p or -c (to the standard output), -j (junk
       paths), -x (exclude), -u or -f (update, freshen), -v, -L, -C,
       -P (password), and no wildcards in names.

       Encrypted entries, Zip64 archives and methods other than
       stored and deflated are refused. An archive comment longer
       than about 1 KiB hides the archive.

       Entry names are limited to 255 characters.
